  "summary": "Nanopore single-molecule structure readouts and Bayesian rate inference for reactive RNA now offer uncertainty-aware ensemble characterization.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is recovering population-level structural or kinetic properties of nucleic acids from molecule-resolved or count-based measurements, with explicit treatment of heterogeneity. The sm-PORE-cupine study couples chemical probing with direct RNA nanopore sequencing and signal alignment to resolve RNA structure ensembles on individual molecules in a eukaryotic cell. The reactor-simulation preprint instead infers sequence-motif rate equation parameters from ligation counts within a Bayesian framework that returns uncertainty estimates. What is new in this pairing is the combination of per-molecule readouts with alignment or likelihood-based inference to recover ensemble properties rather than single consensus states. Coupling measurement noise to explicit parameter uncertainty is the common methodological element across these two items.",
